==English==

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:der|la:resiliēns<id:rebounding>|id=flexible|tree=1}}
From {{der|en|la|resiliēns|alt=resiliēns, resilientem|t=rebounding|pos=present {{lg|active}} {{lg|participle}} of {{m|la|resiliō}}}} (whence {{suf|en|-ent}}), from {{m|la|saliō||leap, jump}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈzɪl.jənt/|/ɹɪˈzɪli.ənt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-resilient.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Simplificationalizer-resilient.wav|a=New Jersey}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Returning quickly to original shape after force is applied; [[elastic]]. {{q|of objects or substances}}
## {{lb|en|materials science}} Having the ability to absorb energy when deformed.
# Returning quickly to normal after damaging events or conditions. {{q|of systems|organisms or people}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1994|author=Michael Grumley|title=The Last Diary
|passage=He’s '''resilient''', and strong, but sometimes tonight, here, the weight of what he’s saying makes him stop, pause as if lost.}}
## {{lb|en|psychology|neuroscience}} Having the ability to recover from [[mental illness]], [[trauma]], etc.; having [[w:resilience (psychology)|resilience]].

